A||N||1||FieroJoe||What body filler to use?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||04-05-2001||11:23 PM||||In the process of taking all the really crappy pinstriping off my coupe to repaint, I ended up gougeing the plastic a little bit.  The bonehead who repainted it years ago before I got it clearcoated right over the stripes, making them a PITA to get off and I had to get out the razor blade.<P>So, I definatly going to have to use some sort of filler.  Should I use regular bondo, or can that pop right out if the panel flexes too much.  Or should I use lacquer putty (green 3M stuff)?  It stays a little softer when totally dry than bondo does.<P>Anybody else run into this problem?<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||04-06-2001||12:05 AM||||use the red putty in tube. the green is mostly for enamel, stays softer than the red. let it dry at least 4 hours at room temp before you sand it or it will shrink when you paint it. Best Ive found is PPG DFL 17 spot putty. scuff sand area around it with 320 sandpaper before you apply it. dont have to get all the paint off.You can also put it on rite over primer.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
